import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Module: hnl_engine_test","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"api/engine-test/hnl_engine_test/hnl_engine_test.md","filePath":"api/engine-test/hnl_engine_test/hnl_engine_test.md"}`),a={name:`api/engine-test/hnl_engine_test/hnl_engine_test.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<p><strong>hnl_engine_test</strong></p><h1 id="module-hnl-engine-test" tabindex="-1">Module: hnl_engine_test <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-hnl-engine-test" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: hnl_engine_test”">​</a></h1><h2 id="contents" tabindex="-1">Contents <a class="header-anchor" href="#contents" aria-label="Permalink to “Contents”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Modules</strong></p><ul><li><a href="#config"><code>config</code></a> - Run configuration: the planner/policy seam (ADR-0089 §&quot;Planner +</li><li><a href="#events"><code>events</code></a> - Structured run events (ADR-0089 §&quot;Observability: structured events from</li><li><a href="#framework"><code>framework</code></a> - # Test framework assert side-channel (ADR-0092)</li><li><a href="#provider"><code>provider</code></a> - Test providers (ADR-0089 §&quot;Providers (extensible from day one)&quot;).</li><li><a href="#report"><code>report</code></a> - Reporting pipeline (ADR-0089 §&quot;Reporting pipeline&quot;).</li><li><a href="#runner"><code>runner</code></a> - Bootstrap test runner (ADR-0089).</li></ul><hr><h2 id="module-config" tabindex="-1">Module: config <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-config" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: config”">​</a></h2><p>Run configuration: the planner/policy seam (ADR-0089 §&quot;Planner + Scheduler&quot;).</p><p>[<code>RunConfig</code>] bundles everything the run stage needs — the selection [<code>Filter</code>], the [<code>SchedulePolicy</code>] for the generic <code>BoundedScheduler</code>, an optional [<code>TestRunCache</code>] (<code>--cache</code>), and an event sink — so the <code>hnl test --filter/--jobs/--cache</code> flags map onto a single entry point, [<code>run_with_config</code>].</p><p>The existing [<code>crate::runner::run</code>], [<code>crate::runner::run_with_filter</code>], [<code>crate::runner::run_selected</code>], and [<code>crate::runner::run_selected_cached</code>] functions are thin wrappers over this entry point.</p><h2 id="module-events" tabindex="-1">Module: events <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-events" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: events”">​</a></h2><p>Structured run events (ADR-0089 §&quot;Observability: structured events from day one&quot;).</p><p>The runner emits a [<code>TestEvent</code>] for every stage transition a consumer might care about: run lifecycle (<code>RunStarted</code>/<code>RunFinished</code>), suite lifecycle (<code>SuiteStarted</code>/<code>SuiteFinished</code>), discovery (<code>TestDiscovered</code>), selection (<code>TestSelected</code>), execution (<code>TestStarted</code>/<code>TestFinished</code>), and outcome refinements (<code>TestCached</code>, <code>TestSkipped</code>, <code>TestFailed</code>).</p><h1 id="collection-not-a-bus" tabindex="-1">Collection, not a bus <a class="header-anchor" href="#collection-not-a-bus" aria-label="Permalink to “Collection, not a bus”">​</a></h1><p>A generic multi-subscriber event bus is explicitly <strong>deferred</strong> until a second consumer appears (ADR-0089 §&quot;Generic infrastructure dependencies&quot; lists &quot;event bus&quot; as a candidate for extraction). Today events are collected into a plain <code>Vec&lt;TestEvent&gt;</code> supplied by the caller — see [<code>crate::config::RunConfig::events</code>] and [<code>crate::provider::TestProvider::discover</code>]. The vocabulary and the collect-then-consume shape are written so that extraction is a move, not a redesign.</p><h1 id="determinism" tabindex="-1">Determinism <a class="header-anchor" href="#determinism" aria-label="Permalink to “Determinism”">​</a></h1><p>Events are emitted in canonical plan order by the run coordinator, never from scheduler worker threads, so parallel execution changes timing but never event ordering (ADR-0089 §&quot;Determinism&quot;).</p><h2 id="module-framework" tabindex="-1">Module: framework <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-framework" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: framework”">​</a></h2><h1 id="test-framework-assert-side-channel-adr-0092" tabindex="-1">Test framework assert side-channel (ADR-0092) <a class="header-anchor" href="#test-framework-assert-side-channel-adr-0092" aria-label="Permalink to “Test framework assert side-channel (ADR-0092)”">​</a></h1><p>The pure-handl test framework now lives in <code>handl-packages/test/</code> and is registered by the canonical kit (<code>kit-canonical</code>). This crate only keeps the thread-local side-channel used by the tree-walk <code>assert</code> keyword handler to store structured failure detail for the runner.</p><h2 id="module-provider" tabindex="-1">Module: provider <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-provider" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: provider”">​</a></h2><p>Test providers (ADR-0089 §&quot;Providers (extensible from day one)&quot;).</p><p>A [<code>TestProvider</code>] turns a source of tests into a [<code>TestPlan</code>]. The runner never cares where tests came from; providers are the seam that lets non-filesystem sources (in-memory buffers, IDE documents, naming conventions) plug in later without modifying the runner.</p><p>Built-in providers:</p><ul><li>[<code>FileSystemProvider</code>] — discovers <code>.hnl</code>/<code>.handl</code> files under the given paths and parses them with the canonical kit ⊕ test-language fragment.</li><li>[<code>ChangedProvider</code>] — wraps [<code>FileSystemProvider</code>] and only surfaces tests in files that changed according to <code>git status --porcelain</code>.</li></ul><p>The future <code>UnitProvider</code>/<code>PropertyProvider</code>/<code>DoctestProvider</code>/… split in the ADR is deferred: discovery currently recognizes all declaration kinds in one pass via data-driven <code>name</code>/<code>body</code> roles (ADR-0081), so splitting by kind is a composition question, not a discovery-mechanism question.</p><h2 id="module-report" tabindex="-1">Module: report <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-report" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: report”">​</a></h2><p>Reporting pipeline (ADR-0089 §&quot;Reporting pipeline&quot;).</p><p>Reports are produced in stages and stay <strong>structured until the very end</strong>:</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Result[] → Summary → Report → Formatter</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li>[<code>summarize</code>] reduces a [<code>RunReport</code>]&#39;s outcomes into a [<code>Summary</code>] (counts, total duration, slowest test).</li><li>A [<code>Reporter</code>] renders <code>(&amp;RunReport, &amp;Summary)</code> into a final string. [<code>HumanReporter</code>] is the console formatter; [<code>JsonReporter</code>] is the machine formatter. Future <code>JunitReporter</code>/<code>TapReporter</code>/<code>CiReporter</code> implement the same trait — <code>--report json|junit</code> changes no execution.</li></ul><p>The existing [<code>crate::runner::format_report_human</code>] and [<code>crate::runner::format_report_json</code>] functions are thin wrappers over these reporters.</p><h2 id="module-runner" tabindex="-1">Module: runner <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-runner" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: runner”">​</a></h2><p>Bootstrap test runner (ADR-0089).</p><p>Discovers, runs, and reports tests written in handl. The runner parses modules with the canonical kit&#39;s Pratt parser, extracts test declarations (bindings whose name starts with <code>test_</code> or test-language <code>test</code>/<code>property</code> etc. forms), and evaluates each test body with the tree-walk eval engine. Failures surface as <code>result.err(...)</code> values from the keyword <code>assert</code> form; the runner catches those and records structured failure detail.</p><h1 id="test-declaration-convention" tabindex="-1">Test declaration convention <a class="header-anchor" href="#test-declaration-convention" aria-label="Permalink to “Test declaration convention”">​</a></h1><p>The v0.1 grammar (ADR-0074) does not yet have a <code>test</code> keyword. The runner uses a <strong>naming convention</strong>: any top-level binding whose name starts with <code>test_</code> is treated as a test case. The test body is the binding&#39;s value expression. For example:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>test_addition = 1 + 1</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>test_failure = assert false</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The first test passes (the expression evaluates without error); the second fails (the assertion returns a <code>result.err</code> value). If the grammar later gains a <code>test</code> keyword, the runner also checks for node kinds containing <code>&quot;test&quot;</code> (see <code>discover_tests_in_module</code>).</p><h1 id="determinism-1" tabindex="-1">Determinism <a class="header-anchor" href="#determinism-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Determinism”">​</a></h1><p>Tests run in source order per module; modules are processed in lexicographic order by path. No shared state between tests (handl has no mutation primitive — this falls out of the language).</p><h1 id="engine-genericity-adr-0081" tabindex="-1">Engine genericity (ADR-0081) <a class="header-anchor" href="#engine-genericity-adr-0081" aria-label="Permalink to “Engine genericity (ADR-0081)”">​</a></h1><p>Child positions are resolved via [<code>Language::child_role_index</code>], never by hardcoded <code>kind == &quot;assign&quot;</code> checks. The runner resolves the <code>target</code> and <code>value</code> roles for each binding node; it falls back to positional children (0 = target, 1 = value) only when no roles are declared.</p>`,43)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};